	uniq - drop repeated lines from a fileSyntax		drop -udc -z=[file] [files..]Description	The uniq command copies lines from its input to its output, comparing 	adjacent lines. The default is to retain a single copy of sets of 	duplicate lines. The input must be sorted for the command to work 	correctly.	The -u option drops all duplicated lines. The -d option retains only 	a single copy of each set of duplicated lines. Default operation is 	effectively the same as using the -du options.	The -c option cannot be combined with -u or -d. It causes each unique	line to be output preceeded with a count of the number of times it	occurs in the input.	Input files may be specified on the command line. If the -z=file	option is present the nominated file contains the list of files to	be processed. If -z does not specify a file the list of files will	be read from the standard input. If no files are specified the 	standard input is taken as the input file.	Output is to the standard output.Bugs	The maximum line length is 160 characters.Author	Martin Gregorie	10 Sadlers Mead	Harlow	Essex	CM18 6HG	Gregorie@LFGMS.logica.com